{"step_index":197,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-01T20:53:17Z","content":"### 1. My Understanding of Your Question\nYou are asking: *\"When can I just be lazy and trust NexLev's estimated revenue dashboard, and exactly when do I need to stop, open vidIQ, run my custom 'RPM Oracle' prompt, and do the math myself?\"* \n\n### 2. What I Am Going to Prove to You\nI am going to prove that NexLev is a \"Western-Biased\" tool. It has a hardcoded assumption that almost every YouTube channel has a large American/Tier 1 audience. Therefore, it only works when that assumption happens to be true. I will give you the exact dividing line of when to trust it and when to ignore it.\n\n### 3. The Blunt Answer: When to Use Which\n\nHere are the strict rules for when to trust NexLev natively, and when to use your custom setup.\n\n#### ✅ WHEN YOU CAN TRUST NEXLEV (Do zero extra work)\nYou can look at NexLev's dashboard and trust its RPM and Revenue numbers **ONLY IF all of these conditions are true:**\n1. **The channel is in English.**\n2. **The content appeals to Western/Global audiences (US, UK, CA, AU).**\n3. **The niche is High/Mid Value:** Tech, Finance, Software, STEM Education, Business, or Global Edutainment.\n\n*Example:* **Byte Sized Explainer.** It's English Tech. NexLev said $8,150. Your custom math said $7,892. NexLev was highly accurate. You don't need to do extra work here.\n\n#### ❌ WHEN NEXLEV WILL LIE TO YOU (You MUST use your custom vidIQ setup)\nYou must ignore NexLev's revenue and run your custom \"RPM Oracle\" prompt **if ANY of these conditions are true:**\n\n1. **The channel is in Hindi (or any regional language like Spanish, Indonesian, Portuguese).**\n   * *Why:* NexLev will hallucinate that 40% of the audience is American and inflate the revenue by 400%. (e.g., NexLev said NeonMan makes $15,000. Reality is ~$4,000).\n2. **The content is strictly localized.**\n   * *Why:* If a channel covers Indian politics, local gossip, or regional news, no Americans are watching it. NexLev will still apply a US RPM.\n3. **The niche is US Entertainment / Pop-Culture.**\n   * *Why:* NexLev *underestimates* these. For channels like Cineporty (Horror) or Stickman 616 (MCU Lore), NexLev drags the RPM down to ~$2.50 because it assumes a random global audience. Your custom setup proves they actually get closer to $3.50-$4.00.\n\n#### ⚠️ SPECIAL CASE: Shorts Channels\n**Always trust NexLev for the VIEW SPLIT.** NexLev is exceptional at separating Long-Form views from Shorts views. vidIQ is terrible at this. If you are analyzing a channel like Vericade (heavy Shorts), look at NexLev to see exactly how many views are Long vs. Short, but use your custom prompt to figure out the actual RPM for those Long-Form views.\n\n### Summary Cheat Sheet\n* **English Tech/Finance/Edu?** -> Just read NexLev.\n* **Hindi / Regional / Gossip?** -> NexLev is wrong. Use your custom vidIQ Prompt. \n* **Pop-Culture / MCU / Horror?** -> NexLev underestimates. Use your custom vidIQ Prompt.","thinking":"**Defining Trust Thresholds**\n\nI'm now zeroing in on the core of the problem: determining the precise conditions under which NexLev's built-in data can be fully trusted, as opposed to requiring the more complex \"vidIQ RPM Oracle + Hybrid Math\" approach. I need to identify key indicators or scenarios.\n\n\n**Clarifying The Core Issue**\n\nI'm now zeroing in on defining when NexLev's default data works well enough, and when it requires the more complex \"vidIQ RPM Oracle + Hybrid Math\" approach. My current question: when is the default data accurate enough, and when does it necessitate the custom vidIQ Master Prompt? I'm focusing on scenarios where the built-in system can fail (e.g., mismatched geography or language). This requires understanding where it works well. For instance, NexLev likely performs best on English channels targeting Tier 1 countries and niches like Tech. This requires me to determine and document the matrix.\n\n\n**Pinpointing Accuracy Limits**\n\nI'm now identifying scenarios where NexLev's default data is inaccurate. I've pinpointed non-English channels and those targeting Tier 3 countries as requiring custom solutions, as the revenue figures can be inflated by 300-500% due to incorrect audience assumptions. Additionally, I'm noting NexLev's underestimation in areas like US Entertainment/Pop-culture/Horror, where a global mix assumption skews RPMs downward. I'm structuring a response to clearly define these boundaries.\n\n\n"}
